src/: the compiler patsopt (ATS2 sources, no GC)
================================================

The compiler is written in ATS2 and compiles itself: ATS1 is not needed to build it.
Any ATS2 compiler can be the first one (stage 0); a bundle of generated C code is the
alternative when there is none (see CBOOT/README). The notes of the port from ATS1 are
in PORTING.md ("src2" there is this directory). The tools built on these modules (atsdoc,
libatsdoc, libatsynmark, pats2xhtml) and the .atxt templates of the repository need no ATS1 either (see utils/atsdoc).

Prerequisites: a C compiler, GNU make, python3 (generates the arena encodings of the
syntax trees, utils/arenagen), libgmp (without it: C3NSTRINTKND=intknd). PATSHOME
defaults to the root of the repository; the prelude, share/ and the C run-time are read
from there.

Building
--------

    make -C src PATSOPT=<stage-0 patsopt> [STAGE0HOME=<its PATSHOME>] [-j N]   # ./patsopt and ./libatsopt.a
    make -C src check                  # typecheck every module (needs only patsopt)
    make -C src test                   # check + unit tests + md5 suites of test/ (no ATS1; no -j; ~6 minutes)
    make -C src CBOOT                  # generate the bootstrap C bundle in CBOOT/ (CBOOT/README)
    make -C src clean | cleanall | cleanboot

`make -f Makefile_devl all` (repository root) does the above and then builds bin/patscc, bin/myatscc and
ccomp/atslib/lib/libatsopt.a and libatslib.a, with the same variables (PATSOPT=, STAGE0HOME=, C3NSTRINTKND=).
Without PATSOPT the stage-0 compiler is $PATSHOME/bin/patsopt if it exists (an earlier build), else CBOOT/patsopt if built, else patsopt of the PATH.
`make -C src test` needs bin/patscc and bin/patsopt in the PATH (build them with Makefile_devl first).

Getting the first patsopt
-------------------------

(a) Any ATS2 compiler as stage 0.

  * A compiler built from this repository (bin/patsopt of an earlier build, a release built from these sources):

        make -C src PATSOPT=/path/to/patsopt          # PATSHOME = this repository

  * An installed ATS2 release (tested: ats2-postiats-0.4.2, Arch/Debian package):

        make -C src PATSOPT=/usr/lib/ats2-postiats-0.4.2/bin/patsopt STAGE0HOME=/usr/lib/ats2-postiats-0.4.2

    That compiler cannot read the prelude of this repository (the prelude without GC has no list.sats, which every
    ATS2 up to 0.4.2 loads itself), so with STAGE0HOME the stage-0 compiler runs with ITS OWN prelude and C run-time
    and only libats/{SATS,DATS,CATS}/arena.* (the arena of the sources) is added from this repository (directory
    stage0/, `-IATS`). `-O2` is replaced by `-O2 -fno-strict-aliasing` in this mode: the C code that ATS2 0.4.2 generates for
    the compiler is not safe with strict aliasing under gcc 16 (the stage-1 compiler is then wrong). Once this
    stage-1 patsopt exists, rebuild with it (`make -C src cleanall; make -C src PATSOPT=$PWD/patsopt`) to get the
    stage-2 compiler, which is the normal one (stage 2 and stage 3 are bit-identical).

(b) The bootstrap C bundle: `make -C src CBOOT` (run with a working patsopt) generates the C code of the whole compiler in
    CBOOT/; `make -C CBOOT` links a patsopt from it with a C compiler and gmp only (CBOOT/README).
    The bundle is tracked (generated files, see .gitattributes): `make bootstrap` at the root builds patsopt from it
    and then the whole of ATS2. Stage-0 order of src/Makefile: PATSOPT=; $PATSHOME/bin/patsopt; CBOOT/patsopt (once built);
    patsopt of the PATH. The root `make` uses the bundle when there is no bin/patsopt and no PATSOPT.

Source conventions
------------------

The no-GC rules of the repository apply to the compiler itself (see CLAUDE.md): linear heap data only, the shared
syntax trees live in arenas (libats/arena) generated from the pats_*.spec files by utils/arenagen/arenagen.py
(the generated pats_*_gen*.hats are not in git; `make -C src check` or the build makes them).
When you add a module add it to the lists of src/Makefile (SATS/DATS and the group lists) and to the dynload list in pats_main.dats.

The ATS1 sources
----------------

The old compiler sources (ATS1) are in the git history (the commit before "src2 renamed to src"). The differential harness
of test/ (the *dump1.sh scripts, `make *compare`, drvfiles/drvopt) compares against an ATS1 build of the old sources:
check them out into a separate directory, build them with ATS1 as before (ATSHOME, ATSHOMERELOC=ATS-0.2.12, make GCFLAG=-D_ATS_NGC
C3NSTRINTKND=gmpknd patsopt), and point ATS1REF at that directory:

    git worktree add /tmp/ats1src <commit before the swap>
    (cd /tmp/ats1src/src && ATSHOME=... make GCFLAG=-D_ATS_NGC C3NSTRINTKND=gmpknd patsopt)
    make -C src/test s2compare ATS1REF=/tmp/ats1src/src

The ATS1 objects only link at the path where they were built (their C names embed the source path); without ATS1REF
the ATS1 side of those targets stops with a message. The md5 suites (`make -C src test`) never need ATS1: they
check against recorded md5 sums of the ATS1 output.

Some md5 files (t1cases.md5, t2cases.md5, t3cases.md5, toolcases.md5 line 1) are md5 sums of outputs that contain the path of the
test files relative to the repository (src/test/...), and the output order depends on it: they are tied to the directory name
src/test. They were regenerated from the ATS1 output after the rename (src2 -> src): `make -C src/test t1compare t2compare
t3filescompare ATS1REF=...` rewrites the first three from the ATS1 output (and checks ATS1 == ATS2), and for toolcases.md5 line 1
`sh toolcases.sh ./tooldump1` (the ATS1 driver, built by `ATS1REF=... sh comdump1.sh tooldump1`) prints it.

